<!-- MATH 14 Skeletal Notes                                                -->
<!-- Section: Line Integrals                                               -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Skeletal (fill-in) version of the section of the same name in the     -->
<!-- completed MATH 14 notes.  The construction of the mass integral (the  -->
<!-- portions, the sum, the limit) is kept as prose, with the right-hand   -->
<!-- side of each of its four displayed equations left blank to fill in;   -->
<!-- the step that trades ds for dt, both worked examples, and every Concept -->
<!-- Check answer are replaced by blank workspace boxes; the two worked    -->
<!-- examples get no instruction (only the labelled rows of the box), the  -->
<!-- other boxes are each preceded by a one-line instruction.  The results -->
<!-- are restated in an assemblage so                                      -->
<!-- that later sections can still cite eq-mass-speed and                  -->
<!-- eq-scalar-line-integral.  File name and xml:ids are unchanged.        -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Figures: kept in full (the partitioned wire and its video, the two    -->
<!-- helical/spiral wires and the mass video, the ds-versus-dr diagram,    -->
<!-- and the square-and-circle wires), since each is either part of a      -->
<!-- problem statement or the check on a box.  The first-quadrant          -->
<!-- reflection picture that answered the charged-wire question is         -->
<!-- replaced by a grid for the student to draw it on.  Captions that      -->
<!-- stated a numerical answer have been trimmed.                          -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Media wiring (relative to the PreTeXt source root):                   -->
<!--   Manim videos : videos/*_1080p.mp4                                   -->
<!--   TikZ figures : embedded inline inside <image><latex-image>          -->
<!--   The TikZ figures need tikz + pgfplots in the <latex-image-preamble> -->
<!--   of main.ptx.                                                        -->

<section xml:id="sec-line-integrals">
  <title>Line Integrals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section we study line integrals of scalar functions. We
      develop the idea through a concrete problem: finding the mass of a
      curved wire when we know its density<mdash/>its mass per unit
      length<mdash/>at every point. The strategy is the one behind every
      integral: <em>chop the wire into tiny pieces, multiply the density of
      each piece by its length, and add up the results</em>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-scalar-line-integrals">
    <title>Line Integrals of Scalar Functions</title>

    <p>
      Suppose we would like to compute the mass of a wire that does not have
      a trivial shape (such as a straight line). If the wire were straight
      and its density constant, the mass would simply be density times
      length. For a curved wire whose density
      <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> varies from point to point, neither shortcut is
      available<mdash/>but we can approximate mass of small portions 
      of the wire by treating each as if it were straight and had constant
      density.
    </p>

    <p>
      Assume the wire is represented by a curve <m>C</m> in space,
      parametrized by <m>t\in[a,b]</m>, as in
      <xref ref="fig-wire-partition"/>. Divide the wire into <m>n</m> small
      portions, and denote the length of the <m>k</m>th portion by
      <m>\Delta s_k</m>. Pick a sample point <m>(x_k,y_k,z_k)</m> on this
      portion. If the portion is short, the density is nearly constant along
      it, so

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-mass-portion" number="yes">
        \text{mass of the } k\text{th portion}
          \approx \rule{6cm}{0.4pt}
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      and summing <xref ref="eq-mass-portion"/> over all portions,

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-mass-sum" number="yes">
        \text{mass of the wire}
          \approx \rule{6cm}{0.4pt}
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-wire-partition">
      <caption>
        A wire represented by a curve <m>C</m> in space, parametrized by
        <m>t\in[a,b]</m> and cut into <m>n=7</m> portions by the partition
        points (black dots). The <m>k</m>th portion (black) has length
        <m>\Delta s_k</m> and contains the sample point
        <m>(x_k,y_k,z_k)</m>, located by the position vector
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t_k)</m>. The red chords form the inscribed polygon
        that approximates <m>C</m>; here its total length falls about
        <m>3\%</m> short of the length of <m>C</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-wire-partition" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>
          A helical wire cut into eight portions by dots, with the inscribed
          polygon of red chords, one portion highlighted in black, and a
          position vector reaching from the origin to a sample point on it.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A helical wire rising counterclockwise around the vertical axis
            from its lower end, marked t equals a, to its upper end, marked t
            equals b. Eight dots cut the wire into seven portions, and a
            straight red chord joins the ends of each portion, so the chords
            form a polygon inscribed in the wire that visibly cuts inside
            every bend. One portion on the near side is drawn in thick black
            and labeled with its length, delta s sub k; a dot inside it is the
            sample point, and a dashed green arrow runs to that dot from the
            origin, labeled r of t sub k equals the vector with components x
            sub k, y sub k, z sub k.
          </p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
              width=11cm, view={115}{20},
              xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
              xmin=-1.35, xmax=1.35, ymin=-1.35, ymax=1.35, zmin=0, zmax=2.1,
              grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
              % labels sit on a light background so they stay legible where
              % they pass near the grid
              label style={font=\Large}, tick label style={font=\normalsize},
              every node/.append style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.75,
                                        text opacity=1, inner sep=1pt,
                                        font=\Large},
            ]
            % the wire C: a helix of pitch 0.3 per radian
            \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:360, samples=200, samples y=0]
                ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {0.3*pi*x/180});
            % the inscribed polygon: seven chords joining the partition points
            \addplot3[red, line width=1.1pt] coordinates {
                (1,0,0) (0.62349,0.78183,0.26928) (-0.22252,0.97493,0.53856)
                (-0.90097,0.43388,0.80784) (-0.90097,-0.43388,1.07712)
                (-0.22252,-0.97493,1.34640) (0.62349,-0.78183,1.61568)
                (1,0,1.88496)};
            % the kth portion of the wire
            \addplot3[black, line width=2.4pt, domain=102.857:154.286,
                      samples=40, samples y=0]
                ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {0.3*pi*x/180});
            % the partition points
            \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, black, mark size=1.7pt] coordinates {
                (1,0,0) (0.62349,0.78183,0.26928) (-0.22252,0.97493,0.53856)
                (-0.90097,0.43388,0.80784) (-0.90097,-0.43388,1.07712)
                (-0.22252,-0.97493,1.34640) (0.62349,-0.78183,1.61568)
                (1,0,1.88496)};
            % the position vector to the sample point
            \addplot3[-{Stealth[length=3.5mm]}, green!55!black,
                      densely dashed, line width=1.6pt]
                coordinates {(0,0,0) (-0.62349,0.78183,0.67320)};
            \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, green!55!black, mark size=2pt]
                coordinates {(-0.62349,0.78183,0.67320)};
            % label placement: each direction and offset below was checked
            % against the projected curves so that no two labels, and no
            % label and curve, overlap
            \node[left=8pt] at (axis cs:1,0,0) {\(t=a\)};
            \node[above right=6pt] at (axis cs:1,0,1.88496) {\(t=b\)};
            \node[blue, below right=9pt]
                at (axis cs:0.28366,-0.95892,1.5) {\(C\)};
            \node[above right=6pt] at (axis cs:-0.62349,0.78183,0.67320)
                {\(\Delta s_k\)};
            \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, black, mark size=1.7pt]
                coordinates {(0,0,0)};
            \node[above left=5pt] at (axis cs:0,0,0) {\(O\)};
            \node[green!55!black, above left=2pt, xshift=14pt, yshift=12pt]
                at (axis cs:-0.31175,0.39092,0.33660)
                {\(\mathbf{r}(t_k)=\langle x_k,\, y_k,\, z_k\rangle\)};
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The estimate <xref ref="eq-mass-sum"/> is only an approximation,
      because the density is not actually constant over each portion. How can
      we make the calculation exact? We take the limit in which every portion
      becomes infinitely small, that is, <m>\Delta s_k\to 0</m>, or
      equivalently <m>n\to\infty</m>:

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-mass-limit" number="yes">
        \text{mass of the wire}
          = \rule{6cm}{0.4pt}
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The limit in <xref ref="eq-mass-limit"/> is denoted by the integral

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-mass-line-integral" number="yes">
        \text{mass of the wire} = \rule{6cm}{0.4pt}
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
       which is read as <q>the line integral of <m>\rho</m> over the curve <m>C</m>.</q> The
      process is animated in <xref ref="fig-wire-partition-video"/>: as the
      partition refines, the sums settle down to the value of the integral.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-wire-partition-video">
      <caption>
        The construction above, on the wire <m>C</m> of
        <xref ref="fig-wire-partition"/>: the wire is cut
        into <m>n</m> portions, and the <m>k</m>th of them, of length
        <m>\Delta s_k</m>, carries a sample point
        <m>(x_k,y_k,z_k)</m>, so its mass is about
        <m>\rho(x_k,y_k,z_k)\,\Delta s_k</m>. As <m>n</m> grows the
        portions shrink, and the sum
        <m>\sum_k \rho(x_k,y_k,z_k)\,\Delta s_k</m> settles down to the
        line integral <m>\int_C \rho\,ds</m>.
      </caption>
      <video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-wire-partition" width="80%"
             source="videos/wire_partition_refine_1080p.mp4"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Our final task is to write the line element <m>ds</m> in terms of
      <m>dt</m>, so the integral becomes an ordinary integral we can
      evaluate.
    </p>

    <sidebyside width="97%">
      <image>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$ds =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {substitute into the mass integral and factor out $dt$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {mass of the wire $=$};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
        <shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the line element in terms of dx, dy and dz, for the substitution into the mass integral with dt factored out, an unlabelled one, and for the resulting formula for the mass.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-line-integral-results">
      <title>Results</title>
      <p>
        For a wire <m>C</m> parametrized by
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle x(t), y(t), z(t)\right\rangle</m>,
        <m>a \le t \le b</m>, with velocity
        <m>\mathbf{v}(t) = d\mathbf{r}/dt</m>, the line element is

      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-line-element" number="yes">
          ds = \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        so the mass of the wire is

      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-mass-speed" number="yes">
          \text{mass of the wire}
            = \int_a^b \rho\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
              \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        There is nothing special about density here. In general, a
        <term>scalar line integral</term> has the form

      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-scalar-line-integral" number="yes">
          \int_C f\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)\,ds
            = \int_a^b f\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
              \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      where <m>f</m> is a scalar-valued function and the curve <m>C</m>
      specifies the region of integration. Once the curve is parametrized in
      terms of <m>t</m>, the region of integration translates to
      <m>a\le t\le b</m>. In words:
      <em>to compute a line integral, trade the geometric element
      <m>ds</m> for the parameter element
      <m>\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\rVert\,dt</m></em>.
      Two sanity checks connect this to what we already know. Taking
      <m>f=1</m> in <xref ref="eq-scalar-line-integral"/> recovers the arc
      length
      <m>\int_C ds=\int_a^b \lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\rVert\,dt</m> of
      <xref ref="eq-arc-length-2d"/><mdash/>total length is the
      <q>mass</q> of a wire of density one. And
      <m>\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\rVert</m> is exactly the speed from that
      section, so the factor converts time spent into distance covered.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE: helical wire. Statement only; solution separate.          -->
  <example xml:id="ex-helix-wire">
    <title>Mass and length of a helical wire</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a wire of length <m>l</m> and mass <m>m</m>. The wire is
        helix-shaped, with its bottom end located at <m>(1,0,0)</m> and
        parametric equation

      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-helix-param" number="yes">
          \mathbf{r}(t)=\langle \cos t,\ \sin t,\ t\rangle,\qquad t\ge 0.
        </mrow>
      </md>
        Its density (mass per unit length) is
        <m>\rho(x,y,z)=4x^2y^2</m> kg/m. Assuming that
        <m>m=\dfrac{\pi}{2\sqrt{2}}</m> kg, calculate the length <m>l</m> of
        the wire.
      </p>
    <p>
      <em>Solution.</em>
    </p>

    <sidebyside width="97%">
      <image>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,17) -- (15,17);
\draw[gray!35] (0,14) -- (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8) -- (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {$\mathbf{v}(t) =$ \quad and \quad $\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\rVert =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.7) {$\rho(x(t),y(t),z(t)) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$m = \displaystyle\int_0^b \cdots =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {solve for $b$ (and why it is the only solution):};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$l =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
        <shortdescription>Blank box in five rows, for the velocity and speed, for the density along the curve, for the mass integral up to the unknown upper limit, for solving for that limit, and for the length of the wire.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
      <figure xml:id="fig-helix-wire">
        <caption>
          The helical wire <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle\cos t,\sin t,t\rangle</m>
          for <m>0\le t\le \pi/2</m>, from its bottom end <m>(1,0,0)</m> to
          its top end <m>(0,1,\pi/2)</m>. The dashed gray curve is its
          shadow on the plane <m>z=0</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-helix-wire" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            A quarter turn of a helix rising from the point (1,0,0) to the
            point (0,1,pi over 2), with its shadow on the floor.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
              minus 1.2 to 1.2 and z from 0 to 1.8. A thick blue curve, one
              quarter turn of a helix, starts at a red point on the floor
              labelled (1, 0, 0), t equals 0, and climbs steadily as it
              curves, ending at a red point labelled (0, 1, pi over 2), t
              equals pi over 2, about 1.57 units above the floor. A dashed
              gray quarter circle on the floor of the box shows the curve's
              shadow.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
                width=10cm, view={115}{20},
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=-1.2, xmax=1.2, ymin=-1.2, ymax=1.2, zmin=0, zmax=1.8,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
              ]
              % shadow on the ground z=0
              \addplot3[gray!50, dashed, thin, domain=0:90, samples=60, samples y=0]
                  ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {0});
              % the helix-shaped wire
              \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:90, samples=80, samples y=0]
                  ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {pi*x/180});
              % endpoints
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.6pt] coordinates
                  {(1,0,0) (0,1,1.5708)};
              \node[anchor=south west, xshift=5pt, yshift=2pt] at (axis cs:1,0,0) {\((1,0,0),\ t=0\)};
              \node[above left=1pt] at (axis cs:0,1,1.5708) {\((0,1,\pi/2),\ t=\pi/2\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-helix-wire-video">
        <caption>
          A point traverses the helical wire while the accumulated mass
          <m>\int_0^t \rho\,\lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert\,d\tau</m> grows. Use
          it to check the mass, the value of <m>b</m>, and the length you
          found.
        </caption>
        <video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-helix-wire" width="85%"
               source="videos/helix_wire_mass_1080p.mp4"/>
      </figure>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE: the spiralling wire, from the companion GeoGebra activity -->
  <example xml:id="ex-spiral-wire">
    <title>A wire whose density is read off the picture</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A wire of length <m>40</m> meters is parametrized as

      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-spiral-wire" number="yes">
          \mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle t\cos(t),\ t\sin(t),\
            \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{3}\,t^{3/2} \right\rangle ,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        where <m>x(t)</m>, <m>y(t)</m> and <m>z(t)</m> are in meters and
        <m>t</m> is in seconds. Assume that the mass density at a point
        <m>P</m> of the wire is

      <md>
        \rho = \frac{1}{d+1} \quad \text{kg/m} ,
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        where <m>d</m> is the distance from <m>P</m> to the
        <m>z</m>-axis.
      </p>
      <p>
        The wire is graphed in <xref ref="fig-spiral-wire"/>. Note that there
        is a piece of information that has to be extracted from the graph.
        Calculate the mass of the wire.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Solution.</em>
      </p>

      <sidebyside width="97%">
        <image>
          <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,17.5) -- (15,17.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,13) -- (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.5) -- (15,2.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {what the graph supplies: the wire is $\ \le t \le b$ because};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,17.2) {$\mathbf{v}(t) =$ \quad and \quad $\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\rVert =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {the upper limit: $40 = $};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$d =$ \quad so \quad $\rho =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$M =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.2) {what the cancellation means:};
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
          <shortdescription>Blank box in six rows, for the starting parameter value read off the graph, for the velocity and speed, for the equation that fixes the upper limit, for the density in terms of t, for the mass integral, and for the meaning of the cancellation.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
      <figure xml:id="fig-spiral-wire">
        <caption>
          The wire <xref ref="eq-spiral-wire"/>, spiralling outward and
          upward from the origin. The dashed gray curve is its shadow on the
          plane <m>z=0</m>. The graph is what tells us the wire starts at
          <m>t=0</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-spiral-wire" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            A wire spiralling outward and upward from the origin, with its
            shadow on the ground.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
              minus 8.5 to 8.5 and z from 0 to 22. A thick blue curve starts
              at a red point at the origin, marked t equals 0, and spirals
              outward and upward, making a little more than one full turn
              around the vertical axis while climbing, its radius growing
              steadily, until it ends high above the floor. A dashed gray
              spiral on the floor of the box shows the curve's shadow.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
                width=11cm, view={120}{18},
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=-8.5, xmax=8.5, ymin=-8.5, ymax=8.5, zmin=0, zmax=22,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
                label style={font=\Large},
                tick label style={font=\normalsize},
                every node/.append style={font=\Large},
              ]
              % the shadow of the wire on the ground z=0
              \addplot3[gray!65, dashed, line width=1.3pt, domain=0:458.37,
                        samples=300, samples y=0]
                  ({pi*x/180*cos(x)}, {pi*x/180*sin(x)}, {0});
              % the wire itself
              \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:458.37,
                        samples=300, samples y=0]
                  ({pi*x/180*cos(x)}, {pi*x/180*sin(x)},
                   {0.942809*(pi*x/180)^1.5});
              % the starting point, which the graph is there to reveal
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.8pt]
                  coordinates {(0,0,0)};
              \node[below right=1pt] at (axis cs:0,0,0) {\(t=0\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- Concept questions, ported from the companion GeoGebra activity     -->
  <!-- "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" (geogebra.org/m/af8gede2).    -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-line-integral-questions">
    <title>Concept Check</title>

    <p>
      This last part of the section is not about computing anything new. It
      is about <em>reviewing</em> the concepts we have just built. Everything 
      below refers
      to the two applications developed so far using line integrals, in particular 
      the length <m>L</m> of a wire and its mass <m>M</m>,

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-question-length" number="yes">
        L \amp = \int_C ds
             = \int_{t=a}^{t=b}\left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt ,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        M \amp = \int_C \rho\left(x,y,z\right) ds
      </mrow>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-question-mass" number="yes">
        \amp = \int_{t=a}^{t=b} \rho\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
                 \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt ,
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      where <xref ref="eq-question-mass"/> is
      <xref ref="eq-mass-speed"/> again and
      <xref ref="eq-question-length"/> is the case <m>\rho = 1</m><mdash/>the
      arc length <xref ref="eq-arc-length-2d"/> once more. Assume
      throughout that the thickness of the wire can be ignored.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="q-line-integral-symbols">
      <title>Reading the two formulas</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="eq-question-length"/> and
          <xref ref="eq-question-mass"/>, what do <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>,
          <m>t</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>dt</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and
          <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> represent?
        </p>
        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$a$, $b$, $t$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$C$, $dt$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {$\mathbf{v}$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {$\rho(x,y,z)$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box with four labelled rows, for the meaning of a, b and t, of C and dt, of the velocity vector, and of the density.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="q-ds-dr">
      <title>The two line elements</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Take two nearby points of the wire, the ones reached at the
          parameter values <m>t_{k-1}</m> and <m>t_k</m>. Write
          <m>\Delta \mathbf{r}
          = \mathbf{r}(t_k) - \mathbf{r}(t_{k-1})</m> for the displacement
          from the first to the second, and <m>\Delta s_k</m> for the length
          of the piece of wire joining them. Intuitively speaking, what do
          <m>ds</m> and <m>d\mathbf{r}</m> represent, and what happens to
          <m>\Delta s_k</m> and <m>\Delta \mathbf{r}</m> in the limit that
          produces them?
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-ds-dr">
          <caption>
            Two nearby points of a curve <m>C</m>, at the parameter values
            <m>t_{k-1}</m> and <m>t_k</m>. The straight arrow is the
            displacement <m>\Delta \mathbf{r}
            = \mathbf{r}(t_k) - \mathbf{r}(t_{k-1})</m>; the thick piece of
            curve joining the same two points has length <m>\Delta s_k</m>. The
            chord is straight and the arc is not, so the two differ<mdash/>but
            less and less as the points come together.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-ds-dr" width="65%">
            <shortdescription>
              A curve with two nearby points, their position vectors from the
              origin, the chord vector between them, and the arc joining them.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A smooth curve rises across the first quadrant. Two points on it,
                reached at the parameter values t sub k minus one and t sub k,
                are joined to the origin by two thin position vectors, labeled r
                of t sub k minus one and r of t sub k. A green arrow runs
                straight from the first point to the second and is labeled delta
                r, the difference of the two position vectors. The piece of curve
                between the same two points is drawn thickly in orange and
                labeled delta s sub k. The straight arrow cuts across inside the
                bend of the curve, so it is visibly shorter than the arc.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="ds-dr">
              <diagram dimensions="(440,360)" margins="5">
                <definition>c(t) = (2.9*cos(t), 1.7*sin(t))</definition>
                <definition>t1 = 0.32</definition>
                <definition>t2 = 1.18</definition>
                <definition>P = c(t1)</definition>
                <definition>Q = c(t2)</definition>
                <definition>D = (Q[0]-P[0], Q[1]-P[1])</definition>
                <definition>M = ((P[0]+Q[0])/2, (P[1]+Q[1])/2)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-0.55,-0.5,3.7,2.7)" aspect-ratio="1">
                  <axes at="ax" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>

                  <group at="curve">
                    <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0.05,1.45)"
                                      stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
                    <label anchor="c(1.42)" alignment="nw" offset="(-8,6)"
                           color="blue">
                      <m>C</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="arc">
                    <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(t1,t2)"
                                      stroke="#d55e00" thickness="5"/>
                    <label anchor="c((t1+t2)/2)" alignment="ne" offset="(10,8)"
                           color="#d55e00">
                      <m>\Delta s_k</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="chord">
                    <vector v="D" tail="P" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"
                            arrow-width="8"/>
                    <label anchor="M" alignment="sw" offset="(-10,-10)"
                           color="#009E73">
                      <m>\Delta \mathbf{r}</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="positions">
                    <vector v="P" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#7a1a99" thickness="2"
                            arrow-width="8"/>
                    <vector v="Q" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#7a1a99" thickness="2"
                            arrow-width="8"/>
                    <point p="P" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                    <point p="Q" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                    <label anchor="0.55*P" alignment="se" offset="(4,-10)"
                           color="#7a1a99">
                      <m>\mathbf{r}(t_{k-1})</m>
                    </label>
                    <label anchor="0.55*Q" alignment="nw" offset="(-8,8)"
                           color="#7a1a99">
                      <m>\mathbf{r}(t_k)</m>
                    </label>
                    <label anchor="P" alignment="se" offset="(8,-6)">
                      <m>t_{k-1}</m>
                    </label>
                    <label anchor="Q" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)">
                      <m>t_k</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="Two nearby points on a curve, with their
                                    position vectors, the straight displacement
                                    between them, and the arc joining them">
                    <annotation ref="ax" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                    <annotation ref="curve" text="The curve C" sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="arc"
                                text="The piece of the curve between the two
                                      points, of length delta s sub k"/>
                    <annotation ref="chord"
                                text="The displacement delta r, a straight arrow
                                      from the first point to the second"/>
                    <annotation ref="positions"
                                text="The position vectors of the two points,
                                      drawn from the origin"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          (Say which of the two is a number and which a vector, and what
          each one records about the step. Then take the limit in
          <xref ref="fig-ds-dr"/>: divide <m>\Delta\mathbf{r}</m> by
          <m>\Delta t_k</m> and let the points come together; do the same
          for the arc, using the fact that the ratio of arc to chord tends to
          <m>1</m>. Which substitution from earlier in the section is this?)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$d\mathbf{r}$ represents \quad / \quad $ds$ represents};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {$\dfrac{\Delta\mathbf{r}}{\Delta t_k} \longrightarrow$ \qquad so \qquad $\Delta\mathbf{r} \longrightarrow$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$\Delta s_k \longrightarrow$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the meaning of the vector and scalar line elements, for the limit of the displacement divided by the time step, and for the limit of the arc length of the step.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="q-units">
      <title>Units</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If we use meters and kilograms as the units of length and mass
          respectively, then what will the units of <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> and
          <m>ds</m> be?
        </p>
        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {units of $\rho$: \qquad\qquad units of $ds$: \qquad\qquad units of $\rho\,ds$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box with one row, for the units of the density, of the line element, and of their product.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="q-integrand">
      <title>What is being integrated?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the above applications of line integrals of scalar functions
          <m>\int_C f(x,y,z)\,ds</m>, what is the scalar function that is
          being integrated (the integrand) along the wire?
        </p>
        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {for the mass, $f =$ \qquad\qquad for the length, $f =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box with one row, for the integrand of the mass integral and the integrand of the length integral.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <figure xml:id="fig-square-circle-wires">
      <caption>
        Two wires: the circle of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin, and
        the square inscribed in it, whose vertices
        <m>\left(\pm\sqrt{2},\pm\sqrt{2}\right)</m> lie on the circle.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-square-circle-wires" width="55%">
        <shortdescription>
          A circle of radius two centered at the origin with a square
          inscribed in it, the square's four vertices lying on the circle.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A coordinate grid showing two closed curves. The first is a
            circle of radius two centered at the origin, passing through the
            marked point two comma zero. The second is a square inscribed in
            that circle, with horizontal and vertical sides and with its four
            vertices, at plus or minus root two in each coordinate, lying on
            the circle. The square's sides therefore cut across the inside of
            the circle.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="square-circle-wires">
          <diagram dimensions="(360,360)" margins="5">
            <definition>R = 2</definition>
            <definition>h = 1.4142135624</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-2.9,-2.9,2.9,2.9)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

              <group at="circle-wire">
                <circle center="(0,0)" radius="R" stroke="blue"
                        thickness="3"/>
                <label anchor="(0,-R)" alignment="sw" offset="(-10,-6)"
                       color="blue" clear-background="yes">
                  <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="square-wire">
                <polygon points="[(h,h), (-h,h), (-h,-h), (h,-h)]"
                         closed="yes" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
                <label anchor="(-h,h)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)"
                       color="#009E73" clear-background="yes">
                  <m>\left(-\sqrt{2},\sqrt{2}\right)</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="marks">
                <point p="(R,0)" size="4.5" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                <label anchor="(R,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>(2,0)</m>
                </label>
                <point p="(h,h)" size="4" stroke="#009E73" fill="#009E73"/>
                <label anchor="(h,h)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,4)"
                       color="#009E73" clear-background="yes">
                  <m>\left(\sqrt{2},\sqrt{2}\right)</m>
                </label>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="A circle of radius two centered at the origin
                                together with the square inscribed in it,
                                whose four vertices lie on the circle">
                <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                <annotation ref="circle-wire"
                            text="The circular wire, the circle of radius two
                                  centered at the origin"
                            sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="square-wire"
                            text="The square-shaped wire inscribed in the
                                  circle, with horizontal and vertical sides
                                  of length two root two"/>
                <annotation ref="marks"
                            text="The point two comma zero on the circle and
                                  the vertex root two comma root two shared by
                                  the square and the circle"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="q-circular-wire-mass">
      <title>A mass with no parametrization</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume that the density of the circular wire in
          <xref ref="fig-square-circle-wires"/> is given by
          <m>\rho(x,y) = \dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2+1}</m>. <em>Without
          parametrizing</em>, find its mass.
        </p>
        <p>
          (What is <m>x^2+y^2</m> <em>along the curve</em>? Then the
          integrand is a constant, and what is left is the length
          <xref ref="eq-question-length"/> of the wire.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {along $C$, $\rho =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {$M = \displaystyle\int_C \rho\,ds =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for the value of the density along the circle and for the mass as that constant times the length.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="q-radius-independence">
      <title>Does the radius matter?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume that the mass density of a circular wire of radius <m>R</m>
          centered at the origin is given by
          <m>\rho(x,y) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m>. Which statement is correct?
        </p>
        <ol marker="A.">
          <li>
            <p>
            The mass of the wire is independent of its radius <m>R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The mass of the wire increases as <m>R</m> increases.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The mass of the wire decreases as <m>R</m> decreases.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          (Circle your choice and justify it: what is <m>\rho</m> on the
          circle of radius <m>R</m>, and what does that make <m>M</m>?)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {on the circle, $\rho =$ \qquad so \qquad $M =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box with one row, for the density on the circle of radius R and the resulting mass.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="q-square-vs-circle-mass">
      <title>Which wire is heavier?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume that the density of the square-shaped wire and of the
          circular wire graphed in
          <xref ref="fig-square-circle-wires"/> is given by
          <m>\rho(x,y) = x^2+y^2</m>. Then, without parametrizing the curves,
          choose the correct option below. Which statement is correct?
        </p>
        <ol marker="A.">
          <li>
            <p>The circular wire is heavier.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The square-shaped wire is heavier.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The two wires have the same mass.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          (Circle your choice and justify it. On the circle,
          <m>x^2+y^2</m> is constant, so its mass is that constant times its
          circumference. For the square you do not need the exact mass: the
          square is inscribed in the circle, so bound <m>x^2+y^2</m> on the
          square from above and multiply by its perimeter. Is the bound
          strict?)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2) -- (15,2);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$M_{\text{circle}} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {$M_{\text{square}} \lt$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,1.7) {conclusion:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the mass of the circular wire, for the upper bound on the mass of the square wire, and for the conclusion.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="q-charge-symmetry">
      <title>A charged wire</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume that the charge density of the square-shaped wire and of the
          circular wire graphed in
          <xref ref="fig-square-circle-wires"/> is given by
          <m>\rho(x,y) = \left|x\right| - \left|y\right|</m>. Then, without
          parametrizing the curves, choose the correct option(s) below.
          Select all that apply.
        </p>
        <ol marker="A.">
          <li>
            <p>The circular wire is negatively charged.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The square-shaped wire is positively charged.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
            In both cases (square and circular wires) the amount of
            positive charge is equal to the amount of negative charge.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          (Draw the quarter of each wire lying in the first quadrant, where
          <m>\rho = x - y</m>, together with the line <m>y = x</m>, and mark
          where the density is positive and where it is negative. Now
          reflect in that line, that is, interchange <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
          what does the reflection do to each shape, to lengths, and to
          <m>\rho</m>? Conclude what the charge on that quarter is, and then
          on the whole wire.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
          <image>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.6, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (0,0) grid (2.5,2.5);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {1,2} \draw (\x,-0.06) -- (\x,0.06) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2} \draw (-0.06,\y) -- (0.06,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Empty first-quadrant grid running from zero to two and a half in each direction, for drawing the quarter of the circular wire, the quarter of the square wire, and the line y equals x.</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (7,5.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (7,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,7.7) {sign of $\rho$ below and above $y = x$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,5.2) {under the reflection, $\rho(y,x) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,2.7) {conclusion, and which option(s):};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the sign of the density on either side of the line y equals x, for the effect of the reflection on the density, and for the conclusion.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

</section>
